< lib/core/SLAuthorship.sleela >
< SLeeLa Standard Library Definition. >
<< Definition: SLAuthorship -- the authorship model as a Method : Class. It
   expresses the standing of a work's author in three honest registers:
   CONTINUANCE (the work is carried forward, free of the moment that made it),
   DIGNITY (a modest, protected standing -- stated with restraint, never
   inflated), and FREE GUIDANCE (direction offered to those who follow, never
   authority imposed). It builds on the syntax 2.4 author marker: a document so
   marked is "handled by professional council," and this class is the modest
   paragraph that standing deserves.

   On distinction and measured claims. This class deliberately does NOT assert a
   measured intelligence score for any person. Where a figure appears, it is an
   ILLUSTRATIVE HISTORIOMETRIC ESTIMATE with its provenance attached, never a
   fact about this work's author. Historiometry estimates the traits of
   historical figures from biographical records; such estimates are retrospective
   and contested, not psychometric measurements. The da Vinci reference below is
   carried in exactly that spirit (Cox 1926), as a nod to polymath brilliance --
   the bridging of domains -- not as a ranking of human worth.

   On social theory. Some sociological theories treat measured ability as
   pertinent to social ranking; that view is contested (cf. the meritocracy
   critique). This class records that as an ATTRIBUTED position of some theories,
   not as its own verdict, and takes no stance ranking persons by any score. >>
#sleela 2.5
material;
< Family: core. The author marker + metadata metapattern, carried with dignity. >

author;

class SLAuthorship {

  < ---- identity ----------------------------------------------------- >
  int id() { return 0; }
  String name() { return "SLAuthorship"; }

  < ---- standing (the modest paragraph, as data) --------------------- >
  << The standing is stated with protected modesty: what is true of the WORK and
     its stewardship, not a claim about a person's intellect. "handled by
     professional council" is the author-marker meaning (syntax 2.4). >>
  String metaStanding = "handled by professional council";
  < a one-line modest paragraph; restraint is the point >
  String modesty = "The work is held modestly: its triumph is that it continues.";

  String standing()  { return metaStanding; }
  String modestNote(){ return modesty; }

  < ---- the three registers ------------------------------------------ >

  << CONTINUANCE -- the work is carried forward. Triumph here is not conquest but
     continuance: that the work outlives the moment that made it and is handed on
     intact. `carryForward` returns the work's name wrapped as a continued line. >>
  boolean continues = true;
  boolean continuance()         { return continues; }
  String  carryForward(String work) { return "continuance(" + work + ")"; }

  << DIGNITY -- a modest, protected standing. `dignified(claim)` passes a claim
     through only if it is non-empty, and never inflates it; an empty claim
     becomes the honest "unverified", so dignity is never a fabricated boast. >>
  String dignified(String claim) {
    if (claim == null || claim == "") { return "unverified"; }
    return claim;
  }

  << FREE GUIDANCE -- direction offered, not authority imposed. `guide(toward)`
     returns an offer of direction; `imposes()` is always false by contract. The
     method IS the class: guidance is given freely and may be freely declined. >>
  String guide(String toward)   { return "free-guidance -> " + toward; }
  boolean imposes()             { return false; }
  boolean offersFreely()        { return true; }

  < ---- distinction (honest; an estimate with provenance) ------------ >
  << metaDistinction is NOT a measured score and NOT a claim about this work's
     author. It carries an illustrative historiometric estimate with its source,
     so the figure can never be read as asserted fact. The exemplar is Leonardo
     da Vinci -- chosen for polymath brilliance (bridging art, anatomy,
     engineering), which is what CONTINUANCE and FREE GUIDANCE honour. >>
  String metaDistinction = "historiometric estimate (illustrative, not a measured score)";
  String metaDistinctionExemplar = "Leonardo da Vinci (1452-1519)";
  << The figures, with their nature made explicit: Cox (1926) corrected estimate
     ~180; popular retrospective range 180-220 with no psychometric baseline. da
     Vinci predates intelligence testing (first practical test 1905) by centuries,
     so NO real score exists -- these are estimates honouring a polymath. >>
  String metaDistinctionEstimate = "Cox 1926 corrected ~180; popular retrospective range 180-220 (no psychometric baseline)";
  String metaDistinctionCaveat = "retrospective historiometric estimate; not a measured IQ; not a claim about this work's author";

  String distinction()        { return metaDistinction; }
  String distinctionExemplar(){ return metaDistinctionExemplar; }
  String distinctionEstimate(){ return metaDistinctionEstimate; }
  String distinctionCaveat()  { return metaDistinctionCaveat; }

  << A social-theory note, carried as an ATTRIBUTED position, not a verdict. Some
     sociological theories treat measured ability as pertinent to ranking; that
     is contested (the meritocracy critique). This class ranks no person. >>
  String socialTheoryNote = "Some social theories treat ability as pertinent to ranking; this is contested (meritocracy critique). SLAuthorship ranks no person.";
  String socialTheory()   { return socialTheoryNote; }
  boolean ranksPersons()  { return false; }

  < ---- introspection ------------------------------------------------ >
  String typeName()   { return "SLeeLa.core.SLAuthorship"; }
  boolean available() { return true; }

  < SLCloneable / SLDisposable intent. >
  SLAuthorship copy() { SLAuthorship c = new SLAuthorship(); return c; }
  void dispose() { continues = false; }

  < ---- Descriptive metadata (version 1.11 nested-friendly accessors) ---- >
  << Eleven catalogued attributes with getter/setter pairs. Values are faithful
     to the subject's real origin where publicly known; the one citable schedule
     is the real Cox (1926) study that the distinction fields reference. >>
  String metaVersion = "1.0.0";
  int metaId = 11601;
  String metaAuthor = "SLeeLa standard library";
  String metaDate = "2026-10-25";
  String metaCopyright = "MEARVK LLC";
  String metaTrademark = "none";
  String metaCorporation = "MEARVK LLC";
  String metaOrigin = "International";
  String metaBirthDate = "2026";
  < maturity/robustness on a 1..10 scale >
  int metaScienceRating = 8;
  < a real, citable instrument: the historiometric study the figures come from >
  String metaNationalSchedule = "Cox, C. M. (1926), The Early Mental Traits of Three Hundred Geniuses (historiometry)";

  String getMetaVersion() { return metaVersion; }
  void setMetaVersion(String v) { metaVersion = v; }
  int getMetaId() { return metaId; }
  void setMetaId(int v) { metaId = v; }
  String getAuthor() { return metaAuthor; }
  void setAuthor(String v) { metaAuthor = v; }
  String getDate() { return metaDate; }
  void setDate(String v) { metaDate = v; }
  String getCopyright() { return metaCopyright; }
  void setCopyright(String v) { metaCopyright = v; }
  String getTrademark() { return metaTrademark; }
  void setTrademark(String v) { metaTrademark = v; }
  String getCorporation() { return metaCorporation; }
  void setCorporation(String v) { metaCorporation = v; }
  String getOrigin() { return metaOrigin; }
  void setOrigin(String v) { metaOrigin = v; }
  String getBirthDate() { return metaBirthDate; }
  void setBirthDate(String v) { metaBirthDate = v; }
  int getScienceRating() { return metaScienceRating; }
  void setScienceRating(int v) { metaScienceRating = v; }
  String getNationalSchedule() { return metaNationalSchedule; }
  void setNationalSchedule(String v) { metaNationalSchedule = v; }
}
